Advertise the EGL device type HarmonyOS asks ANGLE for.

build-4d5ad954 gave the GLES backend a display on OHOS and changed
nothing on a device: still glImplementationParts (gl=none,angle=none),
and on the phone not one GL or EGL line in the log.

That fix was right and never ran. Chromium asks for --use-angle=gles-egl
as EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_OPENGLES_ANGLE together with
EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_DEVICE_TYPE_EGL_ANGLE (ui/gl/gl_display.cc), and
ANGLE validates that attribute before it creates anything:

    case EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_DEVICE_TYPE_EGL_ANGLE:
        if (!clientExtensions.platformANGLEDeviceTypeEGLANGLE)
        {
            val->setError(EGL_BAD_ATTRIBUTE, ...);
            return false;

and Display::GetClientExtensions() only sets that under
ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX -- which ohos-angle-vulkan-backend.patch stopped
OHOS defining. So eglGetPlatformDisplay returned EGL_NO_DISPLAY at
validation, CreateDisplayFromAttribs was never reached, and the failure
Chromium logs for it is a VLOG(1): silent in a release build. That is
the whole of what the phone showed.

Display.cpp has six places that choose by ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX. Four
choose a backend and were handled -- three because an #error made the
compiler insist, and the GLES one in ohos-angle-gles-display.patch. The
other two decide what the display advertises, fail silently, and are
this patch: the EGL device type above, and EGL_MESA_platform_surfaceless,
which OHOS also had while it was taken for Linux. Both are restored to
exactly what 52d6a0b7 had, the last build that rendered.

Nothing outside Display.cpp on this path chooses by that macro: libEGL,
libGLESv2 and the GL backend have no such branch.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after ohos-angle-gles-display.patch.

diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Display.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Display.cpp
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Display.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Display.cpp
@@ -2296,5 +2296,8 @@
 #endif
 
-#if defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX) && (defined(ANGLE_ENABLE_OPENGL) || defined(ANGLE_ENABLE_VULKAN))
+// OHOS had these while it was taken for Linux, and the build that last
+// rendered had them; it is still the same EGL underneath.
+#if (defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX) || defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_OHOS)) && \
+    (defined(ANGLE_ENABLE_OPENGL) || defined(ANGLE_ENABLE_VULKAN))
     extensions.platformSurfacelessMESA = true;
 #endif
@@ -2343,5 +2346,8 @@
 #endif
 
-#if defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX)
+// Chromium's --use-angle=gles-egl asks for exactly this device type, and
+// eglGetPlatformDisplay rejects it at validation unless it is advertised --
+// before CreateDisplayFromAttribs, and so before any backend is chosen.
+#if defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX) || defined(ANGLE_PLATFORM_OHOS)
     extensions.platformANGLEDeviceTypeEGLANGLE = true;
 #endif
